WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.720
On weekdays the street was very lively.
平日里，这条街十分热闹。

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:11.310
It woke to its work about seven o’clock, at the time when the newsboys made their appearance together with the day laborers.
大约七点钟，它醒来开始一天的营生，这时报童们和打零工的劳工一起出现了。

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:36.870
The laborers went trudging past in a straggling file—plumbers’ apprentices, their pockets stuffed with sections of lead pipe, tweezers, and pliers; carpenters, carrying nothing but their little pasteboard lunch baskets painted to imitate leather; gangs of street workers, their overalls soiled with yellow clay, their picks and long-handled shovels over their shoulders; plasterers, spotted with lime from head to foot.
劳工们排成稀稀拉拉的一列，拖着沉重的脚步走过——管子工的学徒，口袋里塞满了一截截铅管、镊子和钳子；木匠，什么也不带，只拎着漆得像皮革的小小纸板饭篮；一伙伙修路工，工装裤上沾满黄泥，肩上扛着镐和长柄铁锹；泥水匠，从头到脚溅满了石灰。

00:00:37.670 --> 00:01:01.740
This little army of workers, tramping steadily in one direction, met and mingled with other toilers of a different description—conductors and swing men of the cable company going on duty; heavy-eyed night clerks from the drugstores on their way home to sleep; roundsmen returning to the precinct police station to make their night report; and Chinese market gardeners teetering past under their heavy baskets.
这支小小的劳工大军迈着沉重的步子，稳稳地朝一个方向走去，与另一类劳作者相遇、交汇——缆车公司去上班的售票员和车厢工；从药房下夜班、睡眼惺忪地回家睡觉的店员；回辖区警察局作夜间汇报的巡警；还有在沉重的菜筐底下摇摇晃晃走过的中国菜农。

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:08.590
The cable cars began to fill up; all along the street could be seen the shopkeepers taking down their shutters.
缆车开始坐满了人；整条街上都能看到店主们在卸下店门的护板。

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.720
Between seven and eight the street breakfasted.
七点到八点之间，这条街吃早饭。

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:20.630
Now and then a waiter from one of the cheap restaurants crossed from one sidewalk to the other, balancing on one palm a tray covered with a napkin.
不时有某家廉价饭馆的侍者从街这边的人行道穿到那边，一只手掌上平托着盖了餐巾的托盘。

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:24.490
Everywhere was the smell of coffee and of frying steaks.
到处飘着咖啡和煎牛排的香味。

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:37.210
A little later, following in the path of the day laborers, came the clerks and shop-girls, dressed with a certain cheap smartness, always in a hurry, glancing apprehensively at the powerhouse clock.
稍后，跟在劳工们后面来的是职员和女店员，打扮得有几分廉价的时髦，总是行色匆匆，忐忑不安地瞥着发电站的大钟。

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:51.790
Their employers followed an hour or so later—on the cable cars for the most part—whiskered gentlemen with huge stomachs, reading the morning papers with great gravity; bank cashiers and insurance clerks with flowers in their buttonholes.
他们的雇主大约一个钟头后跟着到来——大多坐着缆车——蓄着胡须、大腹便便的绅士们，神情十分庄重地读着晨报；还有扣眼里插着花的银行出纳和保险公司职员。

00:01:52.590 --> 00:02:03.420
At the same time the school children invaded the street, filling the air with a clamor of shrill voices, stopping at the stationers’ shops or idling a moment in the doorways of the candy stores.
与此同时，小学生们涌上街头，尖声的吵嚷充满了空气，他们在文具店前停下，或在糖果店门口逗留片刻。

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:17.000
For over half an hour they held possession of the sidewalks, then suddenly disappeared, leaving behind one or two stragglers who hurried along with great strides of their little thin legs, very anxious and preoccupied.
他们占据人行道半个多钟头，然后突然消失了，只留下一两个落在后头的，迈着细细的小腿大步匆匆赶路，神情焦急而心事重重。

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:26.780
Toward eleven o’clock the ladies from the great avenue a block above Polk Street made their appearance, promenading the sidewalks leisurely, deliberately.
快到十一点时，波尔克街往上一个街区那条大街上的太太们出现了，在人行道上悠闲、从容地漫步。

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.390
They were at their morning’s marketing.
她们是来做上午的采买的。

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.900
They were handsome women, beautifully dressed.
她们是些漂亮的女人，衣着华美。

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.240
They knew by name their butchers and grocers and vegetable men.
她们叫得出自家的肉贩、杂货商和菜贩的名字。

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:48.470
From his window McTeague saw them in front of the stalls, gloved and veiled and daintily shod, the subservient provision men at their elbows, scribbling hastily in the order books.
麦克提格从窗口看见她们站在货摊前，戴着手套和面纱，穿着精致的鞋子；卑躬屈膝的食品商贩站在她们身边，在订货簿上匆匆记着。

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:53.710
They all seemed to know one another, these grand ladies from the fashionable avenue.
这些来自那条时髦大街的贵妇人似乎彼此都认识。

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:09.340
Meetings took place here and there; a conversation was begun; others arrived; groups were formed; little impromptu receptions were held before the chopping blocks of butchers’ stalls or on the sidewalk around boxes of berries and fruit.
到处有人碰面；一场交谈开始了；又有人来了；人们聚成一堆一堆；在肉摊的砧板前，或在人行道上围着一箱箱浆果和水果，举行着临时的小型聚会。

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.100
From noon to evening the population of the street was of a mixed character.
从中午到傍晚，街上的人形形色色。

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:24.220
The street was busiest at that time; a vast and prolonged murmur arose—the mingled shuffling of feet, the rattle of wheels, the heavy trundling of cable cars.
这时街上最热闹；一阵巨大而经久不息的嘈杂声升起——混杂着拖沓的脚步声、车轮的辘辘声、缆车沉重的隆隆声。

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:31.200
At four o’clock the school children once more swarmed the sidewalks, again disappearing with surprising suddenness.
四点钟，小学生们又一次挤满人行道，然后又以惊人的速度突然消失了。

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:40.460
At six the great homeward march commenced; the cars were crowded, the laborers thronged the sidewalks, the newsboys chanted the evening papers.
六点钟，声势浩大的归家行军开始了；车厢里挤满了人，劳工们挤满了人行道，报童们拖着腔调叫卖晚报。

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:47.040
Then all at once the street fell quiet; hardly a soul was in sight; the sidewalks were deserted.
接着，街上一下子安静下来；几乎不见人影；人行道上空空荡荡。

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.010
It was supper hour.
是吃晚饭的时候了。

00:03:49.810 --> 00:04:02.170
Evening began; and one by one a multitude of lights, from the demoniac glare of the druggists’ windows to the dazzling blue-whiteness of the electric globes, grew thick from street corner to street corner.
夜晚降临了；一盏又一盏的灯，从药房橱窗那妖魔般的强光，到电灯球那耀眼的蓝白色，从一个街角到另一个街角，渐渐密集起来。

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:04.610
Once more the street was crowded.
街上又一次挤满了人。

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.550
Now there was no thought but for amusement.
此刻人们一心只想着消遣。

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:14.260
The cable cars were loaded with theatergoers—men in high hats and young girls in furred opera cloaks.
缆车里满载着去看戏的人——戴高礼帽的男人和披着毛皮镶边观剧斗篷的少女。

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:37.130
On the sidewalks were groups and couples—the plumbers’ apprentices, the girls of the ribbon counters, the little families that lived on the second stories over their shops, the dressmakers, the small doctors, the harness makers—all the various inhabitants of the street were abroad, strolling idly from shop window to shop window, taking the air after the day’s work.
人行道上三五成群、成双成对——管子工的学徒、缎带柜台的姑娘、住在自家店铺二楼的小家庭、裁缝、小医生、马具匠——这条街上形形色色的居民全都出来了，从一个橱窗悠闲地逛到另一个橱窗，在一天的工作之后出来透透气。

00:04:37.930 --> 00:04:44.950
Groups of girls collected on the corners, talking and laughing very loud, making remarks upon the young men that passed them.
一群群姑娘聚在街角，大声说笑，对从身边经过的小伙子评头论足。

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.850
A band of Salvationists began to sing before a saloon.
一队救世军开始在一家酒馆前唱起歌来。

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.360
Then, little by little, Polk Street dropped back to solitude.
然后，波尔克街渐渐重归沉寂。

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.680
Eleven o’clock struck from the powerhouse clock.
发电站的大钟敲响了十一点。

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:58.850
Lights were extinguished.
灯火熄灭了。

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:03.580
At one o’clock the cable stopped, leaving an abrupt silence in the air.
一点钟，缆索停了，空气中骤然一片寂静。

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:06.120
All at once it seemed very still.
突然间，一切显得非常寂静。

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:14.080
The only noises were the occasional footfalls of a policeman and the persistent calling of ducks and geese in the closed market.
唯一的声响是偶尔传来的警察的脚步声，以及关了门的市场里鸭和鹅不停的叫声。

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.220
The street was asleep.
这条街睡着了。

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:21.200
Day after day, McTeague saw the same panorama unroll itself.
日复一日，麦克提格看着同样的全景画卷自行展开。

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.270
The bay window of his Dental Parlors was for him a point of vantage from which he watched the world go past.
他“牙科诊所”的凸窗是他的有利位置，他从那里看着世界从眼前经过。
